Now Part 7: the particle update, the renderAt function (which composes everything), the state machine, the UI wiring, and the audio.

Let me write the particle update functions:

```js
function updateSpray(sys, sd, kin, t, intensity) {
  // for each particle i
  //   period P = 0.05
  //   e = floor((t - sd[i].ph*P)/P)*P + sd[i].ph*P
  //   age = t - e
  //   if (age < 0 || age > life) hide
  //   emit point: the car's rear wheel contact positions at time e
}
```

To get the car's pose at time e, I need carKinematics(splineS, splineD, e). That's ~5 vector allocations per call — with 260 particles × 2 cars = 520 calls per frame, each allocating several Vector3s → GC pressure. Let me optimize: write a lighter function that only computes the position and the forward vector without allocations, using preallocated temporaries.

Let me write `kinAt(splineS, splineD, time, out)` where out is a reusable object:
```js
const _kinTmp = {pos:new Vector3(), fwd:new Vector3(), right:new Vector3(), up:new Vector3(), s:0, v:0, a:0};
function kinAt(splineS, splineD, time, out) {
  const si = splineS.eval(time);
  const d = splineD.eval(time).v;
  const s = si.v;
  C(s, _p); N(s, _n); T(s, _t);
  out.s = s; out.v = si.d; out.a = si.dd;
  out.pos.copy(_p).addScaledVector(_n, d);
  out.fwd.copy(_t);
  out.right.copy(_t).cross(UP).normalize();
  out.up.copy(out.right).cross(out.fwd).normalize();
  return out;
}
```
And use a pool of kinematics objects to avoid overwriting. For the particle update, I need the kinematics at the emission time e for each particle — different e per particle, so I need a temp per call (but sequential, so one temp is enough).

Note: spline.eval allocates an object {v,d,dd} each call → 520 allocations/frame for spray + more. That's acceptable in JS (small short-lived objects), but let me reduce by lowering the particle count... 260 spray particles per car is reasonable. Actually let me reduce the evaluation cost: for the spray, the emission times are quantised to P=0.05 s, so there are only ~20 distinct emission times in a 1-second window! I can cache per frame: compute the kinematics for each distinct emission time once (20 per car). Since e_i = floor((t - ph_i*P)/P)*P + ph_i*P, and ph_i varies per particle, each particle has its own phase → distinct e_i values. Hmm, ph_i ∈ [0,1) so ph_i*P spans [0, P) → the e_i values are multiples of P offset by ph_i*P → all distinct.

Alternative: quantise the phases to a limited set. Let me instead assign phases from a fixed set of N_phase = 24 slots: ph_i = (i % 24)/24 + small jitter. Then there are only 24 distinct emission times per frame → I can compute the kinematics for each of the 24 slots once per frame and reuse. 

Let me restructure: for each car, the spray pool has SPRAY_N particles; the particle's slot = i % NSLOT (NSLOT=26); phase = slot/NSLOT + jitter(±0.15/NSLOT). The emission time for a slot = floor((t - phase*P)/P)*P + phase*P. With a per-particle jitter, the e_i differ slightly per particle but I can just use the slot's canonical phase (no jitter) for the kinematics and add the jitter only to the drift direction. Then the emission time per slot is computed once per frame, and all the particles in that slot share the same emission point (they differ in their drift randomness) ✓.

So: NSLOT slots, SPRAY_N = NSLOT * K particles (K = 10 per slot, each with different jitter/phase offsets). Hmm, but if all particles in a slot have the same emission time, then K particles are emitted simultaneously at the same point — that's fine (a burst), and with different drift directions it looks like a spray cloud. But the emission would be "all at once" every 0.05 s — visually that's a strobe at 20 Hz. Since each particle has a lifetime of ~0.6 s, we'd have 12 bursts visible → a continuous stream ✓.

Good, that's efficient. Let me use NSLOT=28 slots for spray, with 10 particles per slot = 280. The emission period P=0.05 → 20 emissions/s → 28 slots covers 1.4 s of trail. 

Let me restructure the particle data: for each particle i: slot = i % NSLOT, jitter (jx, jy, jz), scale.

And the emission time for slot k: e_k = floor((t - phase_k*P)/P)*P + phase_k*P where phase_k = k/NSLOT.

Wait: this makes the emission times quantised in a way that depends on the phase offset. Let me verify: with phase_k = k/NSLOT and P the period, e_k = P * floor(t/P - k/NSLOT) + k*P/NSLOT. Since k*P/NSLOT < P, e_k = P*floor(t/P) - P*floor(k/NSLOT)... let me just compute: floor((t - phase*P)/P) = floor(t/P - phase) → an integer m. e = m*P + phase*P. So the emissions for consecutive slots are spread within one period ✓, and the pattern repeats every P ✓.

The age = t - e ∈ [0, P) for the slot whose e is the most recent... no: for each slot, e is the most recent emission for that slot, so age ∈ [0, P). That means each slot only shows particles with an age < P → the trail would be invisible (all particles are fresh). That's wrong.

I need multiple generations: each slot should have several generations alive simultaneously. With the pool of K particles per slot, I can assign them to different generations: particle i in slot k has generation g = floor(i/NSLOT); its emission time = e_k - g*P (i.e., an older generation) → age = (t - e_k) + g*P. With K=10 and P=0.05, the max age = 0.5 s ✓.

So: age_i = (t - e_k) + g*P where g = floor(i/NSLOT).

The emission position for particle i = the car's pose at (t - age_i) = e_k - g*P ✓ (a time in the past).

Good. Let me define:
- SLOTS = 26, PER_SLOT = 11 → 286 particles per system per car.
- The emission period P depends on the system: spray 0.05, smoke 0.07, spark 0.035, shimmer 0.12.

For each system, the per-frame work: 26 slots × 1 kinematics eval = 26 evals per car per system → 4 systems × 2 cars × 26 = 208 evals/frame ✓ cheap.

Implementation:
```js
function updateParticleSystem(sys, sd, kinFn, t, cfg) {
  // cfg: {slots, per, period, life, ...}
}
```

Let me write a generic emitter:

```js
function emitStream(sys, sd, splineS, splineD, t, cfg) {
  const P = cfg.period;
  const kin = _kinTmp2;
  for (let k = 0; k < cfg.slots; k++) {
    const phase = (k + 0.5) / cfg.slots;
    const m = Math.floor((t - phase * P) / P);
    const eBase = m * P + phase * P;
    for (let g = 0; g < cfg.periods; g++) {
      const idx = k + g * cfg.slots;
      if (idx >= sys.count) break;
      const e = eBase - g * P;
      const age = t - e;
      ...
    }
  }
